WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.385
The secret of life lies within a molecule.

2
00:00:03.645 --> 00:00:05.084
For more than a century,

3
00:00:05.344 --> 00:00:07.588
we moved from discovering it to rewriting it.

4
00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:14.506
In 1869, Miescher isolated a new substance
from cell nuclei. He called it nuclein.

5
00:00:14.766 --> 00:00:17.592
No one yet knew what it would mean.

6
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.791
In 1944, transformation experiments
by Avery and colleagues showed that

7
00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:25.241
DNA can transmit hereditary traits.

8
00:00:25.501 --> 00:00:28.347
The clues to life pointed to this molecule.

9
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.434
Drawing on diffraction studies
by Franklin and others,

10
00:00:33.694 --> 00:00:37.066
Watson and Crick proposed
the double-helix model.

11
00:00:37.326 --> 00:00:41.464
Complementary base pairing also suggested
how genetic information could be copied.

12
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.051
In the 1960s, scientists gradually
deciphered the genetic code:

13
00:00:49.311 --> 00:00:53.564
information is transcribed from DNA into RNA,
then translated into protein.

14
00:00:53.824 --> 00:00:56.593
The four letters began to reveal
the language of life.

15
00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:02.314
In 1973, Cohen, Boyer and colleagues
constructed recombinant plasmids.

16
00:01:02.574 --> 00:01:05.987
DNA fragments from different sources
could be combined,

17
00:01:06.247 --> 00:01:08.562
accelerating the development
of genetic engineering.

18
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:14.849
In 1977, Sanger sequencing made
the order of DNA letters easier to read.

19
00:01:15.106 --> 00:01:19.600
Scientists began turning genetic information
into data they could analyze.

20
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:26.920
From 1990 to 2003, the Human Genome Project
assembled a reference map.

21
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.585
A view of roughly three billion base pairs
came into focus,

22
00:01:30.845 --> 00:01:32.683
although some gaps remained.

23
00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:40.418
In 2012, scientists demonstrated
a programmable CRISPR cutting tool.

24
00:01:40.678 --> 00:01:42.447
Guide RNA helps locate the target.

25
00:01:42.707 --> 00:01:46.217
Cas9 cuts the DNA,
and the cell repairs it.

26
00:01:46.477 --> 00:01:49.744
This gave researchers a more flexible tool
for editing genes.

27
00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:56.086
In 2023, the first CRISPR therapy
received approval in the United States.

28
00:01:56.346 --> 00:01:58.490
The power to rewrite also calls
for understanding the consequences.
